The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).

## Summary

The specification required rejecting creation of an owner whose normalized telephone collides with any existing owner, responding with **409 Conflict**.

**Change made** — a single, minimal edit to `addOwner` in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:

```java
Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);
if (this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
        .anyMatch(existing -> existing.getTelephone().equals(owner.getTelephone()))) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.CONFLICT);
}
this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);
```

**Why this keeps structural impact low:**
- **One file, one method touched.** No new dependencies, no constructor change, no service/repository/interface/advice edits — each of those would have paid the WMC of a large class (`ClinicServiceImpl`, the advice) as pure overhead.
- **Reuses existing normalization.** `Owner.setTelephone` already strips non-digits, so both the incoming owner (mapped via the setter) and stored owners are already normalized — no normalization logic to add.
- **Reuses the existing `findAllOwners()` facade method** rather than adding a new query method to the repository/service.
- The `spring-data-jpa` profile's `findAll` runs a JPQL query, which auto-flushes the pending insert, so the just-created owner is visible to the second create within the same transaction — confirmed by the passing `coreRejectsDuplicateTelephone` test, while `functionalityAllowsDistinctTelephone` confirms distinct numbers still succeed.
